bda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{16}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{9}{16}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{9}{16}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4})(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/4}+Bx{e}^{-3x/4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-130143/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130143"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection/43bf9a213bde4929f48856940336b7efb3e6797fab1294bc2c904869db5d073e" :answer/id #uuid "a36d6561-f5dc-524b-be19-95c868cefc97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection/b1e3a51a5ba0396e963fa12bb594de792d207ddd073014843ecad444fd189b48" :answer/id #uuid "89fb276f-32ab-5271-8aae-4e7ff2ef0dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection/8f8bed6e07dedcde5c565d6fe1c8238d78ebc1570f689496042b41e0f20b1162" :answer/id #uuid "8f48b359-c71d-578f-8d32-95ff1693448f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection/c168c401154e25929734fc79de7503647200e4a94088e060cbf7c5b13e0f81e6" :answer/id #uuid "79ed494b-edcd-50fa-96a4-1a633791db28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection/612106baf3d6eef9e18829259641b75ed64c4bf2c9dc7023c9c3ede2e1895af8" :answer/id #uuid "870c019d-6b13-52f0-8cd8-ccc05b8c6631" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56f9e2d7-016a-5b77-aab8-c2a7d47210af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130147/selection/612106baf3d6eef9e18829259641b75ed64c4bf2c9dc7023c9c3ede2e1895af8" :answer-field/choices ["q-130147/selection/43bf9a213bde4929f48856940336b7efb3e6797fab1294bc2c904869db5d073e" "q-130147/selection/b1e3a51a5ba0396e963fa12bb594de792d207ddd073014843ecad444fd189b48" "q-130147/selection/8f8bed6e07dedcde5c565d6fe1c8238d78ebc1570f689496042b41e0f20b1162" "q-130147/selection/c168c401154e25929734fc79de7503647200e4a94088e060cbf7c5b13e0f81e6" "q-130147/selection/612106baf3d6eef9e18829259641b75ed64c4bf2c9dc7023c9c3ede2e1895af8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130147" :question/id #uuid "a2a1553e-9f74-54ad-8a0c-dff5b3ac1feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130147" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and $x{e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130147/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130147"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64798"] :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-90965/selection/b965d747e56da4502f3cc6fd618bafc52cc91c63e77d245fa9272467cd9c1792" :answer/id 