"Given the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and $x{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-3x}+3x$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90965/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-90965"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130146/selection/30f050d26a6fb0963fb9d1700d54909f3670ae944862742fb7cb386d41dabaa0" :answer/id #uuid "e72f0bc6-486c-52c5-afbc-f75c3211f833" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130146/selection/658bcb82b6b349c70fc208622f1c33837a88eb1a22aaccf2aab91fd2e02e62eb" :answer/id #uuid "29a24edf-ef90-57e0-88de-873f1117c593" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130146/selection/e385483c87d801526b5e8735fd1e7208abafba07ff58abffc67129891d644a3e" :answer/id #uuid "584c8a30-8f6b-55f5-8a65-f22fc5aff02e" :answer/type :answer.type