10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5x}$ and $x{e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130146/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130146"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection/6db403e71dc6091b391ced3d485514dc8f279ab8c0fa4cc0e5ea001473ec35e2" :answer/id #uuid "21a13ea9-1a1b-5317-b98b-56e9cf73c558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection/481c36c305bee679d41611f5ca07231e1905d616d7dfab0e236126dd794addf1" :answer/id #uuid "f10aa9c6-8e6f-505d-8208-4dc41a526738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10x{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection/d912075f2b9c915320ea2682ead571929ab4aabd33365baba582fbaae2fece62" :answer/id #uuid "fb4a396f-05c5-54f4-b8d0-23d512aa57dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection/cbb444dd1bf3943e7181d434a4dc5f11b78ea4be77fcfe61460d1030513dcf5b" :answer/id #uuid "1cdf1f26-c527-54c6-8267-aae2bd0bf94e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection/3cfc37555b45474bca59343f3eb852185b4badc20a4810c8e85e3beea7b20194" :answer/id #uuid "f5698ffd-9ef1-566a-bb05-6d7ab7bcbc13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "165f98eb-f0db-5de9-87d2-0079815d2dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130150/selection/d912075f2b9c915320ea2682ead571929ab4aabd33365baba582fbaae2fece62" :answer-field/choices ["q-130150/selection/6db403e71dc6091b391ced3d485514dc8f279ab8c0fa4cc0e5ea001473ec35e2" "q-130150/selection/481c36c305bee679d41611f5ca07231e1905d616d7dfab0e236126dd794addf1" "q-130150/selection/d912075f2b9c915320ea2682ead571929ab4aabd33365baba582fbaae2fece62" "q-130150/selection/cbb444dd1bf3943e7181d434a4dc5f11b78ea4be77fcfe61460d1030513dcf5b" "q-130150/selection/3cfc37555b45474bca59343f3eb852185b4badc20a4810c8e85e3beea7b20194"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130150" :question/id #uuid "c0fdbf32-7538-564e-a800-f0db1bc2ea85" :question/math-academy-id "q-130150" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -4)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and $x{e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130150/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130150"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6591"] :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0.$ If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =  1$  is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6592"] :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+36{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =  6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6593"] :question/problem "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$  for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+36{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root.  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x},$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
